<Docs slug="chat">
  <h1>Chat</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Talk to an agent in a chat instead of a terminal. The main chat runs your board across projects, a task can
    open as a chat, and any project can have one of its own. Turn it on under <strong>Settings &rarr; Chat</strong>.
    It is experimental.
  </p>

  <img
    class="docs-screenshot"
    src="/assets/screenshots/chat.png"
    alt="A task open as a chat, its work folded into one line above the agent's reply"
    loading="lazy"
  />

  <h2>The main chat</h2>
  <p>
    It lives under <strong>Chats</strong> in the sidebar and reaches every project. It can create, start and move
    tasks, and read what their agents are doing. It follows the tasks it starts, and a message arrives when one
    changes column, finishes a turn, waits on an approval, or a hook fails. Choose which under
    <strong>Wake on</strong>. When something has to run on your computer, it asks, and you press Run or Decline.
    Start a new conversation from the sidebar. Each keeps its own folder for notes and files.
  </p>

  <h2>Tasks as chats</h2>
  <p>
    Under <strong>Project Settings &rarr; Tasks &rarr; Open in</strong>, choose <strong>Chat</strong>, and a task
    that starts opens as a chat with the agent you pick. <strong>Setup</strong> runs first, in a tab beside the
    chat, and <strong>First message</strong> goes out once it succeeds, with <code>$OUIJIT_</code> variables filled
    in. A task can hold several chats and terminals: <strong>New chat</strong> and <strong>New terminal</strong>
    are in its menu, and <strong>Earlier chats</strong> reopens one.
  </p>

  <h2>A chat on a project</h2>
  <p>
    The chat button beside the terminal button, or <strong>New chat</strong> in a project's menu, opens a chat in
    the project's own checkout, with no task. Ask it how something works without naming the project first.
  </p>

  <h2>Carrying a conversation on</h2>
  <p>
    <code>ouijit chat open --task &lt;n&gt; --from &lt;chat-id&gt;</code> starts a new chat with an earlier one's
    conversation so far, its most recent part if it is long.
  </p>

  <h2>Following</h2>
  <p>
    <code>ouijit chat follow &lt;task&gt;</code>, <code>ouijit chat unfollow</code> and
    <code>ouijit chat following</code> change which tasks the main chat hears about.
  </p>

  <h2>Where Chat runs</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Run in</strong> puts the main chat on your computer (Host), in Ouijit's sandbox under its own
    profile, or under a Custom launcher of its own (<code>ouijit settings chat set --launcher &lt;cmd&gt;</code>).
    Sandboxed, everything it starts runs in its sandbox too, each task writing only its own worktree. Chat can
    steer only the agents running there. <strong>Ouijit CLI access</strong> is <strong>Whole board</strong> by
    default, which starts, moves and messages tasks. <strong>View</strong> only reads and follows.
  </p>

  <ChatSandboxAccess />

  <h2>Agents</h2>
  <p>Claude Code, Codex, Pi, OpenCode, or any ACP agent.</p>
</Docs>
